  "name": "reaction-wheel-1d",
  "$comment": "Plan 9.7 (X5.2): a cube balanced on one edge by a reaction wheel. The cube is the project-library InvertedPendulumBody (the stdlib Inertia with the gravity torque m*g*l*sin(theta)); its two ports sit on the same absolute rotation: AngleSens and SpeedSens read the first, the motor housing is on the second. The motor and wheel are the stdlib drives/ReactionWheel (R 1 ohm, L 0.1 mH, k 0.0335, absolute wheel inertia J_w = 6e-4) instead of a DCMotor plus an Inertia: the DCMotor composite has no stator reaction (STATUS NOTE-8), so a cube driven through it is pushed the wrong way and angular momentum is not conserved; ReactionWheel delivers the reaction on the housing and puts the back-EMF on the relative rate. A 2 ms discrete LQR (library/LQR.jl, gains from design/design.py) drives the voltage, clamped to +-12 V. The stdlib Sum takes one vector input that a JSON signal graph cannot assemble from scalar outputs, so the relative wheel speed is the inline RelSpeed (wheel speed minus body speed, like the ErrorSum of the dc-motor sample). The wheel angle for the 3D binding is the drive's observed Drive/phi_rel. AngleSens starts at phi = theta(0) = 0.05 rad.",
